                    text: "ICRA estimated the GST cut lowers AC prices 6–8% (₹2,000–3,000 a unit), more than the new star-label cost. The rules pull the other way on costs. New energy-label tables for ACs, refrigerators and ceiling fans took effect on 1 January 2026, and the next reset is due about January 2029. A quality-control order caps compressor imports at 30% of FY25 volumes, which is why **Voltas** has agreed a compressor joint venture with Atomberg. A robotic-vacuum order due in September 2026 moved **Eureka Forbes**’s robot assembly to Dixon. Higher e-waste recycling prices cost **Whirlpool** ₹143.6cr in FY26 (₹80.6cr a year earlier), and are under industry challenge. Some rules help the leaders: fan energy norms let **Crompton** expect to ‘consolidate the industry at the entry segment’, and BIS rules for air coolers should hurt unorganised metal-body makers that compete with **Symphony**. **My view: regulation is raising the cost of entry, which favours the national brands with engineering teams; it is also a steady margin drag every three years.**",
